# NinFit line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.
#
# Git stored LF, the Windows checkout held CRLF, and nothing in the repository
# said which was correct. The result was ~41 tracked files permanently reported
# as modified while containing no semantic change at all - every real diff had
# to be found inside that noise, and `git add -A` would have committed 10,000
# lines of line-ending churn at any moment.
#
# It was not cosmetic. Several suites assert against component source using
# literals containing "\n" (see src/test/startupCinematic.test.ts). Those
# assertions cannot match CRLF source, so the same commit passed on Linux and
# failed on Windows, which is the most expensive kind of test failure there is.
#
# `core.autocrlf` cannot fix this: it is a per-machine setting, so it is a
# developer preference rather than a repository policy. This file is the policy.
#
# WHAT IT DOES.
#
#   text=auto   Git decides text vs binary, and normalises text to LF on commit.
#   eol=lf      Text is checked out as LF on every platform, Windows included.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate rather than incidental. `text=auto` alone would give
# Git a clean LF history while still leaving CRLF in a Windows working tree -
# which stops the phantom diffs but leaves the "\n" assertions failing locally.
# Checking out LF everywhere is what makes local and CI agree.
#
# Modern Windows tooling - VS Code, Node, Vite, Git for Windows - all handle LF
# working files without complaint. If a genuinely CRLF-only file is ever needed
# (a .bat or .cmd, say), give it its own explicit `eol=crlf` rule below rather
# than weakening the default.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets. Never converted, never diffed as text.
#
# `text=auto` already detects these correctly today. Declaring them means the
# heuristic can never get it wrong about a background WebP, an icon or the intro
# media - a single mistaken conversion would corrupt the file rather than merely
# reformat it, so this is cheap insurance on the one class of error that is not
# recoverable by re-running a formatter.
#
# SVG is deliberately absent: it is XML, it belongs under the text rule, and it
# should diff as text.
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mp3  binary
